1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Fernando del Bosque ----------- Explorer and Colonizer 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT On May 15,1674, the Audiencia of Guadalajara appointed AntonioBalcÃ¡rcel as alcalde mayor (governor) of Coahuila for five years. The new official was charged with exploring and colonizing the province athis own expense. In Novembe
2 CONC r 1674 on a march to thenorth of Saltillo, BalcÃ¡rcel chose Fernando del Bosque, an experienced and trustworthy soldier, as his standard bearer. That expedition founded the settlement of Nuestra Senora de Guadalupe near the site o
2 CONC f present Monclova on December 8, 1674. Associated with the new outpost were twomissionaries, fathers Juan Larios and Dionisio de San Buenaventura,who wished to expand their Christianization efforts to include Indiansnorth of the 
2 CONC Rio Grande. In 1675 BarcÃ¡rcel chose Bosque to accompany an expedition that crossed the Rio Grande on May 11 of that year. The site of the crossing is in dispute, but it was perhaps Paso de Francia(see san antonio crossing), near 
2 CONC the future site of mission San Juan Bautista. The Bosque-Larios expeditionqv traveled forty-one leagues(about 110 miles) beyond the Rio Grande and gave names to sixlocalities. During the journey Bosque wrote an extensive reportdes
2 CONC cribing the topography that they encountered. At several sitesIndians indicated their willingness to accept religious instructioninthe Christian faith. This undertaking and an earlier reconnaissance ofthe same general area by Brot
2 CONC her Manuel de la Cruz is notable in thatthey are the earliest well-authenticated missionary enterprises to cross the Rio Grande below the Pecos junction. After completion of the entrada in June 1675, Bosque suggested that three mi
2 CONC ssion districts,including lands and Indians north of the Rio Grande, be established.Internal conditions in Coahuila delayed action on the proposal for adecade. By then the focus ofattention had shifted to East Texas tocounter the 
2 CONC threat posed by RenÃ© Robert Cavelier, Sieur de La Salle.The French challenge in many respects served to short-circuit early missionary activity in extreme south central Texas. In the late 1670s Fernando del Bosque disappears from
2 CONC  known historical records. Source:Handbook of Texas Online.
2 CONT
2 CONT On April 30, 1675, Fernando del Bosque, Fray Juan Larios, Fray Dionisio de San Buenaventura, ten Spanish soldiers, Lazaro Agustin, governor of the Indian pueblo of San Miguel de Luna, Capt. Juan de laCruz, and twenty Bobole Indian
2 CONC s set out from the mission of Nuestra Senora de Guadalupe, at the site of present Monclova, Coahuila, to convert the Indians of the Coahuila. On the Nadadores River they were joined by a hundred Guyquechale Indians. The expedition
2 CONC  reached theRio Sabinas (Coahuila) on May 5, erected a cross, and took formal possession of the river. On May 11 it reached the Rio Grande, probably a little below the present site of Eagle Pass. Bosque took formal possession of t
2 CONC he river, erected a wooden cross, and renamed the river the San Buenaventura del Norte. On May 15 the expedition was met by several Indian chiefs, who asked the missionaries to come to teach and baptize their followers. The oath o
2 CONC f allegiance to the king was administered to all the Indians, a portable altar was set up, and Mass was celebrated.
2 CONT
2 CONT In all, the Spaniards traveled forty leagues past the Rio Grande and made six halts in south central Texas. On May 25 they reached a sitein present Edwards County that they called San Pablo Hermitano. Theyreturned by a northerly r
2 CONC oute to Guadalupe, where they arrived on June12; there Bosque made a formal report to Antonio de BalcÃ¡rcel. Thelatter recommended that three mission districts be established, including land and Indians north of the Rio Grande. In
2 CONC dian hostilities and disputes with Nuevo Leon about the jurisdiction of the area,however, delayed implementation of the plan for more than a decade, and by that time the focus of efforts had shifted to East Texas to counteract Fre
2 CONC nch incursions. Source: Handbook of Texas Online.
2 CONT
